<TopicPage slug="subjects/neuroscience/advanced/brain-imaging-methods">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Brain imaging is <strong>the bridge between mind and matter</strong> — each method trades
    spatial precision against temporal precision, and the mature researcher knows which question
    each tool can honestly answer. Choosing the method is choosing which kind of truth about the
    brain you can see.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the imaging toolbox and its honest limits" widget="equationStep" config={imagingCfg} />

  <h2>The method-matching discipline</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>fMRI answers "where":</strong> which regions activate during a task. Its subtler use — decoding (predicting what someone saw from activity patterns) — revived questions about mental privacy.</li>
    <li><strong>EEG answers "when":</strong> event-related potentials track processing steps to the millisecond — how the brain recognises a face within 170 ms.</li>
    <li><strong>Diffusion MRI maps the wiring:</strong> white-matter tracts traced by water movement — the "connectome" project, the brain's equivalent of the genome map.</li>
    <li><strong>Optogenetics is the causality tool:</strong> light-activated neurons controlled experimentally in animals (Deisseroth, 2005→) — turning "correlates with" into "causes", the leap imaging can't make alone.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Huettel, Song & McCarthy, Functional Magnetic Resonance Imaging; Bennett et al. ("dead salmon"), 2009; Deisseroth, Nature Methods (2011).</p>
</TopicPage>
